      "_v3_comment": "report_style 'change' switches Lisa to the V3 CHANGE DIGEST (build_change_digest_html), which reports what changed since the previous run instead of re-ranking the whole active inventory every morning. It reads the lifecycle database (jobs_lisa.json), not the lane snapshots, which is what lets it say 'new today' honestly, stop repeating roles past 14 days, and list a role as removed only if that role actually appeared in an earlier email. 'digest' is the previous ranked-inventory renderer and still works; Chad has no report_style and keeps the original lane-by-lane email. max_posting_age_days is now the FETCH/retention window, not the display window — display age is governed by the lifecycle bands (0-7 new, 8-14 still worth applying, 15+ suppressed unless exceptional AND verified open). It is 21 rather than 30 so that a 15-21 day role can still qualify for the verified exception without carrying a month of dead weight. fit_mode stays 'rank' on purpose: not_recommended roles are hidden at render time but retained for the weekly audit, which 'filter' would prevent.",
      "report_style": "change",
      "max_posting_age_days": 21,
      "_discovery_comment": "V3 DISCOVERY GATE — the fix for 'too few genuinely new jobs'. match_groups below is a PRECISION filter and was doing a RECALL job: because its two groups are ANDed, a role had to name both a seniority word and a function word IN ITS TITLE to be retrieved at all, so 'Director, Pipeline Excellence', 'Sr. Product Manager' and 'Program Manager' never reached the model. On one measured day that discarded 130 of 171 leadership-shaped roles. This block runs SECOND, after match_groups: a role that misses the precise gate is still retrieved when its title names a role family AND it carries either a seniority marker or real mandate language in its description. Those roles are tagged tier 'discovery' and, when the model is less confident, land in the email's Discovery / Wildcards section for a human to judge rather than being thrown away. exclude_any here is the ONLY absolute exclusion and is deliberately much shorter than the precision exclude_any below — every term in it is a function Lisa can never want, because each one is a potential false negative.",
